```latex
 \documentclass{article}
 \usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}

 \begin{document}

 Define elements containing two fields ג€“ type, value. Assume that the number of different possible values for the type field is $k$. We want to construct a data structure containing up to $n$ elements that supports the following operations:

 \begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Insert(x)} ג€“ Inserts the element $x$ into the data structure in $O(1)$ time.
  \item \textbf{AddToType(t,a)} ג€“ Increases the value field of all elements of type $t$ by $a$ in $O(1)$ time.
  \item \textbf{AddToElement(a,x)} ג€“ Receives a pointer to the element $x$ and increases its value field by $a$ in amortized $O(1)$ time.
  \item \textbf{Max(t)} ג€“ Returns the maximum element among the elements of type $t$ in $O(1)$ time.
  \item \textbf{MaxAll()} ג€“ Returns the maximum element in the structure (without type restriction) in $O(1)$ time.
 \end{itemize}

 b. Propose such a data structure for up to $k$ different types numbered $1, \ldots, k$.

 \end{document}
 ```

column13:
```latex
 \documentclass{article}
 \usepackage{hebfont}

 \begin{document}
 \textbf{A.} 
 We will use an array with a resizing algorithm as taught in the lecture on amortization ג€“ instead of waiting for it to be full and then transferring everything at once to a larger array, ensure the transfer of information in parts: every time an insert is made after the current array is more than half full, free up two spaces in the new array and transfer two elements to it.

 \textbf{Insert:} As we said ג€“ initially allocate an empty array of fixed size (for example, 4), every insertion after it is more than half-full will free up two spaces in the new array and insert two elements into them. This way, the current array is filled, the new array is exactly half full, and ready for us to work with. A fixed number of operations and therefore \(O(1)\) worst case (the insertions are, of course, Insert-last).

 \textbf{FindByKey:} Go through all the elements of the array until found, or until the array ends. \(O(n)\) W.C..

 \textbf{FindByIndex:} This is an array. \(O(1)\) W.C..
 \end{document}
 ```

